    "question": "What are the main differences between regular functions and arrow functions?",
    "answer": "1) Regular functions have their own 'this' bound dynamically to the caller; arrow functions inherit 'this' lexically from their enclosing scope. 2) Regular functions have an 'arguments' object; arrow functions do not. 3) Regular functions can be constructors with 'new'; arrow functions cannot.",
    "shortExplanation": "Arrow functions provide concise syntax for callbacks and functional methods like map and filter.",
    "codeExample": "const obj = {\n  name: 'Asha',\n  regularFn: function() { console.log(this.name); },\n  arrowFn: () => { console.log(this.name); } // 'this' is window / global, not obj\n};",
    "difficulty": "Easy",
    "questionType": "Concept",
