    "topic": "DOM Debugging",
    "subTopic": "Script Placement",
    "question": "Why does document.getElementById('btn') return null when placed in the <head> tag, and how do you fix it?",
    "answer": "Browsers parse HTML top to bottom. If the script in <head> executes before the browser reaches the <body> and parses the button, the element does not yet exist in the DOM tree.",
    "shortExplanation": "Fix by adding the 'defer' attribute to <script src=\"app.js\" defer>, moving the <script> before </body>, or wrapping code in document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ...).",
    "codeExample": "<!-- Solution 1: Use defer in head -->\n<head>\n  <script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>\n</head>\n\n<!-- Solution 2: DOMContentLoaded in JS -->\ndocument.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {\n  const btn = document.getElementById('btn'); // Now works!\n});",
