    "subject": "React",
    "topic": "Core Concepts",
    "subTopic": "Virtual DOM",
    "question": "What is the Virtual DOM in React and how does reconciliation work?",
    "answer": "The Virtual DOM is a lightweight JavaScript object representation of the real DOM tree kept in memory. When state changes, React creates a new Virtual DOM tree, diffs it with the previous one (reconciliation), and updates ONLY the changed real DOM nodes.",
    "shortExplanation": "Batching DOM updates significantly improves rendering performance compared to touching the real DOM repeatedly.",
    "codeExample": "// React declarative UI:\nfunction App() {\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>;\n}",
