    "num": 3,
    "subject": "React",
    "topic": "Hooks",
    "subTopic": "useState",
    "question": "How does the useState hook work and why should you use an updater function?",
    "answer": "useState declares a state variable and returns a pair: [currentState, setterFunction]. When updating state based on previous state, always pass an updater function (prev => prev + 1) to avoid stale closures during asynchronous state batching.",
    "shortExplanation": "Never mutate state directly (e.g. count = count + 1); always call the setter function.",
    "codeExample": "const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n// Correct way to increment based on previous state:\nconst handleIncrement = () => {\n  setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);\n};",
    "difficulty": "Easy",
    "questionType": "Concept",
    "interviewRound": "Technical Round",
    "frequency": "High",
    "references": "React Docs",
    "followUpQuestions": "What happens if you update state with the exact same value?",
    "addedAt": 65,
