    "topic": "Basics",
    "subTopic": "Runtime & Architecture",
    "question": "What is Node.js and why is it single-threaded?",
    "answer": "Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment built on Chrome's V8 engine that runs JavaScript outside the browser. It uses an asynchronous, event-driven, non-blocking I/O model.",
    "shortExplanation": "It is single-threaded on the JavaScript execution stack, but offloads expensive I/O operations (file, network, DNS) to the OS and C++ threadpool using the libuv library.",
    "codeExample": "// Single thread handles request routing asynchronously:\nconst http = require('http');\nconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => {\n  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });\n  res.end('Hello from Node.js\\n');\n});\nserver.listen(3000);",
