    "question": "What is the difference between WHERE and HAVING clauses in SQL?",
    "answer": "WHERE filters individual rows BEFORE aggregate functions are applied. HAVING filters grouped rows AFTER aggregate functions (SUM, COUNT, AVG) have computed.",
    "shortExplanation": "HAVING can use aggregate functions in its condition; WHERE cannot.",
    "codeExample": "SELECT department, COUNT(*) AS total_emps, AVG(salary) AS avg_sal\nFROM employees\nWHERE status = 'Active'\nGROUP BY department\nHAVING AVG(salary) > 60000;",
