    "subject": "SQL",
    "topic": "Joins",
    "subTopic": "Self Join: Employees Earning More Than Manager",
    "question": "Write a SQL query to find employees who earn more than their direct manager.",
    "answer": "Use a SELF JOIN by joining the Employees table with itself. Alias one instance as 'e' (representing the employee) and the second instance as 'm' (representing the manager) on e.manager_id = m.id, then filter in the WHERE clause where e.salary > m.salary.",
    "shortExplanation": "Self joins allow comparing rows within the exact same table hierarchy.",
    "codeExample": "-- Schema: Employees (id, name, salary, manager_id)\nSELECT \n  e.name AS Employee,\n  e.salary AS EmployeeSalary,\n  m.name AS Manager,\n  m.salary AS ManagerSalary\nFROM Employees e\nJOIN Employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id\nWHERE e.salary > m.salary;",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "questionType": "SQL Query",
    "interviewRound": "Technical Round",
    "frequency": "High",
    "references": "LeetCode 181 - Employees Earning More Than Their Managers",
