    "subject": "SQL",
    "topic": "SQL & Relational Databases",
    "subTopic": "Queries, Joins, Aggregation & Design",
    "question": "Performance Debugging: A query 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE email LIKE %gmail.com' is extremely slow despite an index on email. Why?",
    "answer": "The leading wildcard '%' prevents the B-tree index from seeking from the root node, forcing a full table scan. B-trees only support prefix searches ('john%').",
    "shortExplanation": "",
    "codeExample": "-- Full table scan:\nWHERE email LIKE '%gmail.com'\n-- Index seek supported:\nWHERE email LIKE 'john%'",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "questionType": "Debugging",
    "interviewRound": "Technical Round",
    "frequency": "High",
    "references": "SQL Standard Reference",
    "followUpQuestions": "How do you do fast suffix search?",
