    "subject": "DSA",
    "topic": "Recursion",
    "subTopic": "Base Case & Factorial Implementation",
    "question": "What is recursion and why is a base case necessary?",
    "answer": "Recursion is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve smaller subproblems of the same problem. A base case is the termination condition that stops recursion. Without a base case, the function calls itself indefinitely, exhausting the maximum call stack size and throwing a 'RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded' (stack overflow).",
    "shortExplanation": "Every recursive function can alternatively be written iteratively using a loop and manual stack.",
    "codeExample": "function factorial(n) {\n  if (n <= 1) return 1; // Base case: stops recursion\n  return n * factorial(n - 1); // Recursive call\n}\n\nconsole.log(factorial(5)); // 120 (5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1)",
    "difficulty": "Easy",
    "questionType": "Coding",
    "interviewRound": "Coding Round",
    "frequency": "High",
    "references": "MDN Web Docs - Recursion",
    "followUpQuestions": "What is tail call optimization in recursion?",
