    "topic": "Data Structures & Algorithms",
    "subTopic": "Core Algorithms, Complexity & Trees",
    "question": "What is Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) in a BST?",
    "answer": "In a BST, starting from root: if both p and q are smaller than root, LCA is in left subtree. If both are larger, LCA is in right subtree. If they split, current root IS the LCA!",
    "shortExplanation": "",
    "codeExample": "function lowestCommonAncestor(root, p, q) {\n  if (p.val < root.val && q.val < root.val) return lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);\n  if (p.val > root.val && q.val > root.val) return lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);\n  return root; // Split point is LCA!\n}",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "questionType": "Problem Solving",
    "interviewRound": "Technical Round",
